nd zero after-tax basis, the full $100,000 is added to your taxable income. You can evaluate your marginal rate with our tax bracket calculator."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can married couples filing separately contribute to a Roth IRA?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"If you file separately and lived with your spouse at any time in 2026, your direct Roth IRA contribution phase-out is $0 to $10,000, so your limit is zero once your modified adjusted gross income reaches $10,000. If you file separately and did not live with your spouse at any time in 2026, the single-filer range of $153,000 to $168,000 applies."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I make a backdoor Roth if I make $500,000 a year?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. The IRS pages we checked list no income limit on converting a traditional IRA, and the Form 8606 instructions subtract conversion income when figuring modified adjusted gross income for Roth eligibility. They do not address the backdoor sequence by name."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the downside of a backdoor Roth?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The primary downside of a backdoor Roth IRA is the tax cost triggered if you hold pre-tax IRA balances on December 31 under the pro rata rule. In addition, each conversion carries a separate five-year clock under IRS Publication 590-B regarding the 10% additional tax on early distributions of converted taxable amounts before age 59½. Conversions made after 2017 cannot be undone."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is the backdoor Roth IRA still allowed in 2026?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Publication 590-A allows any traditional IRA owner to convert to a Roth IRA, and the Instructions for Form 8606 explain how to report nondeductible contributions and conversions. The IRS pages we checked on November 18, 2026 do not name the contribute-then-convert sequence or rule on it, so confirm it fits your return with a tax professional."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need to file Form 8606 for a backdoor Roth IRA?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, you must file Form 8606 with your Form 1040 return whenever you make a nondeductible traditional IRA contribution or a Roth conversion. Form 8606 documents your after-tax basis and calculates the taxable portion of your conversion. Failing to file the form when required incurs a $50 IRS penalty unless reasonable cause applies."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I do a backdoor Roth IRA if I already have a traditional IRA?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, but any pre-tax money in your traditional, SEP, or SIMPLE IRAs triggers the IRS pro rata rule on December 31. Under Publication 590-B, the IRS aggregates all your non-Roth IRAs to calculate the taxable percentage of your conversion. Rolling your pre-tax IRA balance into a workplace qualified retirement plan first eliminates that tax exposure."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is this tax advice?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. This guide provides general federal tax information based on 2026 IRS contribution limits and the tax year 2025 Instructions for Form 8606, which represent the current published revision. It does not constitute personal tax, legal, or financial advice, and you should consult a qualified tax professional to evaluate your specific return."}}]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Person","name":"Jonathan Velez","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/about/","jobTitle":"Personal Finance Writer & Editor","description":"Jonathan Velez writes and edits ModernWallet's personal-finance content, focused on loans, interest, and debt payoff. Every calculator page is reviewed against primary sources like the CFPB and Federal Reserve.","knowsAbout":["Auto loans","Loan amortization","Interest and APR","Debt payoff strategies","Personal finance"],"worksFor":{"@type":"Organization","name":"ModernWallet","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com"}}</script> <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"How to Do a Backdoor Roth IRA in 2026","description":"Learn how to do a backdoor Roth IRA in 2026. Follow the five steps, calculate pro rata taxes, report on Form 8606, and avoid costly tax mistakes.","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/guides/backdoor-roth-ira/","image":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/favicon.svg"},"datePublished":"2026-11-18","dateModified":"2026-11-18","author":{"@type":"Person","name":"Jonathan Velez","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/about/"},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"ModernWallet","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/favicon.svg"}},"citation":[{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS Instructions for Form 8606 (tax year 2025)","url":"https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i8606"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS About Form 8606","url":"https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-8606"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS Publication 590-A, Contributions to Individual Retirement Arrangements (IRAs)","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590a"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS Publication 590-B, Distributions from Individual Retirement Arrangements (IRAs)","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS IR-2025-111, 2026 401(k) and IRA limits","url":"https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/401k-limit-increases-to-24500-for-2026-ira-limit-increases-to-7500"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS Retirement topics: IRA contribution limits","url":"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-ira-contribution-limits"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS Roth IRAs","url":"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/roth-iras"}]}</script> <main class="page" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <nav class="crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <a href="/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>ModernWallet</a> <span data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>/</span> <a href="/guides/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Guides</a> <span data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>/</span> <span class="crumb-current" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>How to Do a Backdoor Roth IRA in 2026</span> </nav> <header class="hero" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h1 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>How to Do a Backdoor Roth IRA in 2026</h1> <div class="byline" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr> <span class="by" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>By <a href="/about/" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>Jonathan Velez</a>, Personal Finance Writer &amp; Editor</span> <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>·</span> <span class="rev" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>Reviewed against primary sources · Updated November 18, 2026</span> </div>   <p class="lede" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>To do a backdoor Roth IRA, contribute after-tax money to a traditional individual retirement arrangement (IRA), convert that balance to a Roth IRA, and file Form 8606 with your tax return.</p><p class="lede" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>The strategy moves money into a Roth IRA when your income exceeds federal contribution limits. It works cleanly when you hold zero pre-tax dollars across your traditional, Simplified Employee Pension (SEP), and Savings Incentive Match Plan for Employees (SIMPLE) IRAs on December 31.</p><p class="lede" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>If you hold pre-tax IRA balances on that date, the IRS pro rata rule treats your conversion as a blend of taxable pre-tax dollars and tax-free basis.</p> </header> <section class="tools" aria-label="Tools for this journey" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <span class="tools-title" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Tools for this journey</span> <div class="tool-pills" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <a class="tool-pill" href="/backdoor-roth-pro-rata-calculator/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Backdoor Roth pro rata<span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>→</span></a><a class="tool-pill" href="/tax-bracket-calculator/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Tax bracket<span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>→</span></a><a class="tool-pill" href="/investing/roth-ira-calculator/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Roth IRA<span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>→</span></a> </div> </section>   <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>The Backdoor Roth IRA Steps in Order</h2> <p>The backdoor Roth IRA requires completing five sequential steps during the tax year.</p><p>Before taking action, confirm your IRA balances and workplace retirement plan rules to prevent unexpected taxation.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="prose-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Step</th><th scope="col">Action</th><th scope="col">Form or Account</th><th scope="col">Tax Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">1. Check balances</th><td>Check pre-tax balances across traditional, SEP, and SIMPLE IRAs on December 31 and verify whether your employer plan accepts incoming rollovers.</td><td>Custodial statements and workplace plan documents</td><td>None. Identifying pre-tax money tells you whether the pro rata rule will tax the conversion.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2. Contribute after-tax</th><td>Deposit after-tax money into a traditional IRA up to your annual limit.</td><td>Traditional IRA account</td><td>Nondeductible contribution. You do not claim a tax deduction for these deposited dollars.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">3. Convert to Roth</th><td>Move the traditional IRA balance into a Roth IRA.</td><td>Roth IRA account</td><td>The pro rata rule taxes the converted amount in proportion to pre-tax money across all your traditional, SEP, and SIMPLE IRAs. With no pre-tax IRA money on December 31, the conversion is tax-free.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">4. Report the move</th><td>File Form 8606 with your annual Form 1040 tax return to track nondeductible basis and the conversion.</td><td><a href="https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-8606" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS Form 8606</a></td><td>Establishes your after-tax basis on line 1 to line 3 and figures the taxable amount on line 18.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5. Confirm balance</th><td>Review your Roth IRA deposit confirmation.</td><td>IRA custodial statements</td><td>None. Confirms assets moved successfully into the Roth IRA account.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The single thing to do first is checking your existing traditional, SEP, and SIMPLE IRA balances for pre-tax dollars. If your employer plan accepts incoming rollovers from traditional IRAs, moving pre-tax balances into that plan before December 31 clears the path for a clean conversion.</p><p>For additional timing and distribution constraints, read our guide to <a href="/guides/roth-conversion-rules/">Roth conversion rules</a>. Once the assets sit in your Roth IRA, future qualified growth and distributions come out tax-free.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Who the Backdoor Roth IRA Is For</h2> <p>The backdoor Roth IRA serves high earners whose income exceeds federal thresholds for direct contributions. According to the Internal Revenue Service (IRS) release <a href="https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/401k-limit-increases-to-24500-for-2026-ira-limit-increases-to-7500" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IR-2025-111</a>, the 2026 Roth IRA direct-contribution income phase-out runs from $153,000 to $168,000 for single filers and heads of household. For married couples filing jointly, the phase-out runs from $242,000 to $252,000.</p><p>Married individuals filing separately who lived with their spouse at any time in 2026 face a phase-out of $0 to $10,000. Married individuals filing separately who did not live with their spouse at any time in 2026 use the single-filer range of $153,000 to $168,000. Under <a href="https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-ira-contribution-limits" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS IRA contribution limits</a>, the 2026 annual contribution cap is $7,500, with an additional $1,100 catch-up for individuals age 50 and over bringing their total cap to $8,600. Total contributions to traditional and Roth IRAs combined cannot exceed your taxable compensation.</p><p>The <a href="https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/roth-iras" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS Roth IRAs guidance</a> confirms that direct contributions are not deductible, qualified distributions are tax-free, and you can contribute at any age if you have taxable compensation. When your modified adjusted gross income exceeds the phase-out range, direct Roth IRA contributions drop to zero.</p><p>This strategy is not for earners whose income sits below the phase-out thresholds, because they can contribute to a Roth IRA directly. It is also not suited for individuals holding substantial pre-tax IRA balances that cannot be rolled into a workplace plan, nor for anyone seeking individualized filing advice on a specific tax return.</p><p>If you have workplace retirement options, comparing a <a href="/compare/roth-ira-vs-roth-401k/">Roth IRA vs Roth 401(k)</a> shows that designated Roth 401(k) accounts carry no income restrictions on contributions. You can also review basic account differences in our <a href="/compare/roth-ira-vs-traditional-ira/">Roth IRA vs traditional IRA comparison</a>.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>How the Pro Rata Rule Taxes the Conversion</h2> <p>The pro rata rule requires the IRS to treat all traditional, SEP, and SIMPLE IRAs as a single combined balance when determining what portion of a distribution is taxable. Under <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS Publication 590-B</a> Worksheet 1-1, the nontaxable share equals your total after-tax basis divided by the sum of your December 31 traditional IRA balances plus all distributions and conversions taken during the calendar year.</p><p>In our internal calculation, we ran the Form 8606 proportion on a $7,500 contribution converted in full, assuming federal tax rules only, zero prior basis, no investment growth, and varying pre-tax balances held across traditional, SEP, and SIMPLE IRAs on December 31. You can test your own balances using our <a href="/backdoor-roth-pro-rata-calculator/">backdoor Roth pro rata calculator</a>.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="prose-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Pre-tax IRA balance on Dec 31</th><th scope="col">Basis share (Form 8606)</th><th scope="col">Tax-free part of the $7,500</th><th scope="col">Taxable part</th><th scope="col">Taxable share</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">$0</th><td>1.000</td><td>$7,500.00</td><td>$0.00</td><td>0%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">$25,000</th><td>0.231</td><td>$1,732.50</td><td>$5,767.50</td><td>76.9%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">$92,500</th><td>0.075</td><td>$562.50</td><td>$6,937.50</td><td>92.5%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">$250,000</th><td>0.029</td><td>$217.50</td><td>$7,282.50</td><td>97.1%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In our internal calculation, an individual with a $92,500 pre-tax rollover IRA who makes a $7,500 nondeductible contribution finds that only 7.5% of the conversion is tax-free, leaving $6,937.50 subject to ordinary income tax. As a calculated counterexample, if that individual rolls the $92,500 pre-tax balance into an employer qualified plan before December 31, their year-end traditional IRA balance drops to $0. Under that scenario, the basis share rises to 1.000 and the entire $7,500 conversion becomes tax-free.</p><p>This calculation mirrors the IRS's own illustration in Publication 590-B Worksheet 1-1 featuring Rose Green, where $2,300 of basis against a $20,000 year-end value and a $5,000 conversion yielded a basis share of 0.092, resulting in $460 tax-free and $4,540 taxable. The dollar amounts above reflect taxable income additions rather than final taxes owed, which depend on your tax bracket. You can check your marginal rate with our <a href="/tax-bracket-calculator/">tax bracket calculator</a>.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Reporting It on Form 8606</h2> <p>You must report nondeductible traditional IRA contributions and Roth conversions on Form 8606 attached to Form 1040. Form 8606 references throughout this guide follow the tax year 2025 <a href="https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i8606" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Instructions for Form 8606</a>, which remain the current published revision checked on November 18, 2026. The 2026 form may renumber specific lines.</p><p>In Part I of Form 8606, line 1 records your nondeductible contributions for the year, while line 2 lists your basis carried forward from earlier years. Line 3 sums lines 1 and 2 to establish your total basis. Line 6 asks for the total value of all your traditional, SEP, and SIMPLE IRAs as of December 31, plus any outstanding rollovers. The converted amount itself is not added to line 6, and you do not include outstanding rollovers from a traditional IRA to a qualified employer plan.</p><p>Line 8 records the net amount converted to a Roth IRA, and line 14 tracks your remaining after-tax basis carried forward to future tax years. In Part II, line 16 lists the total amount converted, line 17 enters the basis allocated to that conversion, and line 18 calculates the taxable amount by subtracting line 17 from line 16. The line 18 taxable amount transfers directly to Form 1040 line 4b, while a fully tax-free conversion is reported on line 4a.</p><p>The IRS imposes specific financial penalties for reporting errors on this form. Failing to file a required Form 8606 for a nondeductible contribution carries a $50 penalty unless reasonable cause is shown, and overstating nondeductible contributions on the form results in a $100 penalty.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>What the IRS Says About Conversions and Backdoor Roth IRAs</h2> <p>Under <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590a" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS Publication 590-A</a>, any traditional IRA owner may withdraw assets and reinvest them into a Roth IRA within 60 days as a conversion contribution. In addition, the Instructions for Form 8606 specify that modified adjusted gross income for Roth IRA contribution eligibility is calculated by subtracting Roth IRA conversions reported on Form 1040 line 4b. Consequently, executing a large conversion does not inflate your modified adjusted gross income for direct contribution eligibility.</p><p>Conversions made after 2017 cannot be recharacterized (undone). Publication 590-A also clarifies that required minimum distributions cannot be rolled over or converted to a Roth IRA. If you have reached required distribution age, you must take your mandatory distribution before converting remaining funds.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Backdoor Roth IRA Mistakes That Create Surprise Taxes</h2> <ul class="prose-list"><li>Holding pre-tax IRA balances on December 31: Having deductible IRA, SEP IRA, or SIMPLE IRA balances at year-end triggers the pro rata rule, making a large percentage of your conversion taxable. The fix is rolling the pre-tax balance into an employer plan that accepts rollovers before December 31, if your plan allows it.</li><li>Skipping Form 8606: a missing form for a nondeductible contribution carries a $50 penalty unless you show reasonable cause. File the form with your Form 1040.</li><li>Confusing this process with the mega backdoor route: A regular backdoor Roth IRA uses after-tax contributions to an individual traditional IRA up to the $7,500 limit ($8,600 if age 50 or older), whereas a <a href="/guides/mega-backdoor-roth-401k/">mega backdoor Roth 401(k)</a> relies on after-tax contributions inside an employer workplace plan. The fix is confirming whether your savings originate in an individual IRA or an employer-sponsored retirement plan.</li></ul><p>For readers who also participate in employer plans, understanding <a href="/guides/roth-401k-withdrawal-rules/">Roth 401(k) withdrawal rules</a> helps align your broader retirement withdrawal strategy.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Our Takeaway</h2> <p>A backdoor Roth IRA works best for a high earner locked out of direct Roth contributions who holds zero pre-tax IRA balance across all accounts on December 31. When a substantial rollover or SEP IRA balance exists, the pro rata rule taxes most of the conversion.</p><p>Based on our internal calculation of the pro rata share, an individual with $92,500 in pre-tax traditional IRAs must include 92.5% of a $7,500 conversion in taxable ordinary income. Rolling pre-tax balances into an employer plan that accepts rollovers clears that hurdle, while high earners without that option might consider contributing to a workplace Roth 401(k) where contribution limits carry no income caps.</p><p>Our verdict would change if your workplace plan rejects incoming rollovers, if your annual income falls below the Roth phase-out thresholds to allow direct contributions, or if federal tax legislation alters the conversion framework. To evaluate your specific numbers, model your balances today with our <a href="/backdoor-roth-pro-rata-calculator/">backdoor Roth pro rata calculator</a>.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Frequently asked questions</h2> <div class="faqs" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Can I contribute to a Roth if I make $300,000?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>No, you cannot contribute directly to a Roth IRA if your modified adjusted gross income reaches $300,000. Under IRS IR-2025-111, the 2026 direct contribution phase-out ends at $168,000 for single filers and $252,000 for married couples filing jointly. You can, however, make a nondeductible traditional IRA contribution and convert it through a backdoor Roth IRA.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>How much tax will I pay on a $100,000 Roth IRA conversion?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>The tax you pay on a $100,000 conversion depends on your pre-tax IRA balances and your personal income tax bracket. Under IRS Publication 590-B Worksheet 1-1, if the account holds only pre-tax money and zero after-tax basis, the full $100,000 is added to your taxable income. You can evaluate your marginal rate with our <a href="/tax-bracket-calculator/">tax bracket calculator</a>.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Can married couples filing separately contribute to a Roth IRA?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>If you file separately and lived with your spouse at any time in 2026, your direct Roth IRA contribution phase-out is $0 to $10,000, so your limit is zero once your modified adjusted gross income reaches $10,000. If you file separately and did not live with your spouse at any time in 2026, the single-filer range of $153,000 to $168,000 applies.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Can I make a backdoor Roth if I make $500,000 a year?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Yes. The IRS pages we checked list no income limit on converting a traditional IRA, and the Form 8606 instructions subtract conversion income when figuring modified adjusted gross income for Roth eligibility. They do not address the backdoor sequence by name.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>What is the downside of a backdoor Roth?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>The primary downside of a backdoor Roth IRA is the tax cost triggered if you hold pre-tax IRA balances on December 31 under the pro rata rule. In addition, each conversion carries a separate five-year clock under IRS Publication 590-B regarding the 10% additional tax on early distributions of converted taxable amounts before age 59½. Conversions made after 2017 cannot be undone.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Is the backdoor Roth IRA still allowed in 2026?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Publication 590-A allows any traditional IRA owner to convert to a Roth IRA, and the Instructions for Form 8606 explain how to report nondeductible contributions and conversions. The IRS pages we checked on November 18, 2026 do not name the contribute-then-convert sequence or rule on it, so confirm it fits your return with a tax professional.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Do I need to file Form 8606 for a backdoor Roth IRA?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Yes, you must file Form 8606 with your Form 1040 return whenever you make a nondeductible traditional IRA contribution or a Roth conversion. Form 8606 documents your after-tax basis and calculates the taxable portion of your conversion. Failing to file the form when required incurs a $50 IRS penalty unless reasonable cause applies.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Can I do a backdoor Roth IRA if I already have a traditional IRA?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Yes, but any pre-tax money in your traditional, SEP, or SIMPLE IRAs triggers the IRS pro rata rule on December 31. Under Publication 590-B, the IRS aggregates all your non-Roth IRAs to calculate the taxable percentage of your conversion. Rolling your pre-tax IRA balance into a workplace qualified retirement plan first eliminates that tax exposure.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Is this tax advice?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>No. This guide provides general federal tax information based on 2026 IRS contribution limits and the tax year 2025 Instructions for Form 8606, which represent the current published revision. It does not constitute personal tax, legal, or financial advice, and you should consult a qualified tax professional to evaluate your specific return.</p> </details> </div> </section>  <section class="section sources" data-astro-cid-3ntd4h4x><h2 data-astro-cid-3ntd4h4x>Sources</h2><p class="source-note" data-astro-cid-3ntd4h4x>
